  "difficulty": "intermediate",
  "markdown": "# Karplus-Strong String Synthesis\n\n## Overview\n\nThe Karplus-Strong algorithm (1983) simulates plucked strings using a simple delay-line feedback structure. It's computationally efficient while producing realistic string tones.\n\n## Algorithm\n\n```\nInitial burst (random/noise) → Delay Line → Lowpass Filter → Output\n                                           ↑              ↓\n                                           └──────────────┘\n```\n\n## Core Implementation\n\n```cpp\nclass KarplusStrongOscillator\n{\npublic:\n    KarplusStrongOscillator()\n    {\n        delayLine.resize(maxDelayLength);\n        std::fill(delayLine.begin(), delayLine.end(), 0.0f);\n    }\n    \n    void setup(float frequency, float sampleRate)\n    {\n        // Calculate delay line length (determines pitch)\n        delayLength = static_cast<int>(sampleRate / frequency);\n        delayLength = std::max(2, std::min(delayLength, maxDelayLength - 1));\n        \n        // Calculate filter coefficient (controls decay)\n        // Higher values = longer sustain\n        filterCoefficient = 0.99f;\n        \n        // Initialize with noise burst (pluck)\n        for (int i = 0; i < delayLength; ++i)\n        {\n            delayLine[i] = random.nextFloat() * 2.0f - 1.0f;\n        }\n        \n        readIndex = 0;\n    }\n    \n    float process()\n    {\n        // Read from delay line\n        float sample = delayLine[readIndex];\n        \n        // Apply one-zero lowpass filter (averaging)\n        float filtered = 0.5f * (sample + previousSample);\n        previousSample = sample;\n        \n        // Apply decay\n        filtered *= filterCoefficient;\n        \n        // Write back to delay line\n        delayLine[readIndex] = filtered;\n        \n        // Advance read index\n        readIndex = (readIndex + 1) % delayLength;\n        \n        return sample;\n    }\n    \nprivate:\n    std::vector<float> delayLine;\n    int delayLength = 0;\n    int readIndex = 0;\n    float previousSample = 0.0f;\n    float filterCoefficient = 0.99f;\n    juce::Random random;\n    \n    static constexpr int maxDelayLength = 65536;\n};\n```\n\n## Pitch Calculation\n\nThe fundamental frequency is:\n\n```\nF₀ = Fs / (N + ½)\n```\n\nwhere:\n- `Fs` = sampling frequency\n- `N` = delay line length in samples\n- The extra ½ comes from phase delay of the averaging filter\n\n## Extended Karplus-Strong (Jaffe-Smith)\n\nThe original algorithm has limitations. Extensions add:\n\n### 1. Better Tuning\n```cpp\n// Use allpass filter for precise tuning\nfloat allpassFractional(float input, float delay, float coefficient)\n{\n    float output = coefficient * input + delay - coefficient * delay;\n    return output;\n}\n```\n\n### 2. Adjustable Decay\n```cpp\n// Separate decay for fundamental and harmonics\nvoid setDecay(float fundamentalDecay, float harmonicDecay)\n{\n    // Use different filter types\n    lowpass.setCoefficient(fundamentalDecay);\n    highpass.setCoefficient(harmonicDecay);\n}\n```\n\n### 3. Dynamic Control\n```cpp\n// Dynamics filter for realistic pluck intensity\nvoid setPluckIntensity(float intensity)\n{\n    // Low-pass filter the initial noise burst\n    // Higher intensity = brighter initial spectrum\n    initialNoiseFilter.setCutoff(intensity * 1000.0f);\n}\n```\n\n### 4. Glissando and Slurs\n```cpp\n// Smooth pitch transitions\nvoid glideToFrequency(float targetFreq, float glideTime)\n{\n    targetFrequency = targetFreq;\n    glideRate = (targetFreq - currentFrequency) / (glideTime * sampleRate);\n    isGliding = true;\n}\n\nfloat process()\n{\n    if (isGliding)\n    {\n        currentFrequency += glideRate;\n        if (currentFrequency == targetFrequency)\n            isGliding = false;\n        updateDelayLength();\n    }\n    // ... rest of processing\n}\n```\n\n## JUCE Voice Implementation\n\n```cpp\nclass KarplusStrongVoice : public SynthesiserVoice\n{\npublic:\n    KarplusStrongVoice()\n    {\n        envelope.setAttack(0.001f);\n        envelope.setDecay(0.1f);\n        envelope.setSustain(0.0f);\n        envelope.setRelease(0.5f);\n    }\n    \n    void noteOn(int midiNote, float velocity) override\n    {\n        auto freq = MidiMessage::getMidiNoteInHertz(midiNote);\n        oscillator.setup(freq, getSampleRate());\n        \n        // Velocity affects brightness\n        oscillator.setPluckIntensity(velocity);\n        \n        envelope.noteOn();\n    }\n    \n    void renderNextBlock(AudioBuffer<float>& buffer, int startSample, int numSamples) override\n    {\n        auto* channelData = buffer.getWritePointer(0, startSample);\n        \n        for (int i = 0; i < numSamples; ++i)\n        {\n            channelData[i] = oscillator.process() * envelope.getNextSample();\n        }\n    }\n    \nprivate:\n    KarplusStrongOscillator oscillator;\n    ADSR envelope;\n};\n```\n\n## Variations\n\n### Acoustic Guitar Modeling\n- Use multiple delay lines for sympathetic resonance\n- Add bridge/body resonance convolution\n- Include fret buzz for realism\n\n### Electric Guitar\n- Add distortion simulation\n- Model pickup position\n- Include string coupling\n\n### Harp/Koto\n- Longer decay times\n- Different pluck characteristics\n- Damping simulation\n\n## Performance Considerations\n\n1. **Delay line**: Circular buffer is most efficient\n2. **Filter**: One-zero is cheapest; one-pole adds sustain control\n3. **Polyphony**: Each voice needs its own delay line\n4. **Memory**: Max delay determines lowest playable note\n\n## Comparison to Other Methods\n\n| Method | CPU | Memory | Realism | Control |\n|--------|-----|--------|---------|--------|\n| Karplus-Strong | Low | Medium | Good | Limited |\n| Digital Waveguide | Medium | Low | Excellent | High |\n| Sampled | Very Low | High | Variable | None |\n| Physical Modeling FM | High | Low | Excellent | Very High |\n\n## References\n\n- Karplus, K. & Strong, A. (1983). \"Digital Synthesis of Plucked String and Drum Timbres\"\n- Jaffe, D.A. & Smith, J.O. (1983). \"Extensions of the Karplus-Strong Plucked String Algorithm\"\n- Smith, J.O. \"Physical Audio Signal Processing\" - CCRMA Stanford",
